<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mirwais Hotak</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1673">1673</a>–<a href="/years/1715">1715</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Mir Wais</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mirwais Hotak was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghilzai">Ghilzai</a> Afghan chief from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a> who rebelled against Safavid rule in 1709. He killed the Safavid governor and established an independent Afghan state. His revolt began the Afghan challenge that, under his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-hotak">Mahmud Hotak</a>, brought down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Afghan independence had a forerunner: in 1709 <b>Mirwais Hotak</b>, chief of the Ghilji tribe, broke from the Safavids, and his son Mahmud Hotak ruled much of Iran from 1722 to 1725, until Nader Shah Afshar toppled the Hotaks in 1738.</blockquote><a href="/empire/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1709 the Ghilzai Afghans of Kandahar, Sunnis resentful of Safavid rule, rose under <b>Mir Wais Hotak</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1709 <b>Mirwais Hotak</b>, a chief of the Ghilji Pashtuns, led a revolt in Kandahar against the Safavid governor Gurgin Khan, killed him and made Kandahar independent.</blockquote><a href="/state/hotak-dynasty">Hotak dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.62,65.72&amp;year=1700" aria-label="Kandahar on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6245 1012 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Mirwais Hotak"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6695 1282h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6695 1282h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.62,65.72&amp;year=1700">What was here in 1700? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
